Feature: Customer 360 Raw Table Backup and Vacuum Operations

  This feature covers the backup of the purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw table as compressed parquet files partitioned by state, and the vacuum operation to retain only records from the last 30 days.

  Background:
    Given the source table is purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw
    And the backup location is /Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup/
    And the backup format is parquet with snappy compression
    And the partition column is state
    And all columns from the source table must be included in the backup
    And the retention filter for vacuum uses the creation_date column
    And the retention period is 30 days (720 hours)
    And the current date is <current_date> (format: yyyy-MM-dd)

  Scenario: Successful backup of customer_360_raw table to compressed parquet files partitioned by state
    Given the customer_360_raw table contains records with valid data
    When the PySpark script reads all records from purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw
    And writes the data as parquet files to /Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup/ partitioned by state with snappy compression
    Then parquet files are created in /Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup/ with subfolders for each state value
    And each parquet file contains all columns: id, name, email, phone, company, job_title, address, city, state, country, industry, account_manager, creation_date, last_interaction_date, purchase_history, notes, zip
    And the data types and nullability are preserved
    And no partial or corrupt files are present in the backup location

  Scenario Outline: Error during backup due to invalid backup location
    Given the backup location is "<invalid_path>"
    When the PySpark script attempts to write parquet files to "<invalid_path>"
    Then the script fails with error message "Backup location <invalid_path> does not exist or is not writable"
    And no files are created in "<invalid_path>"

    Examples:
      | invalid_path                      |
      | /Volumes/non_existent_backup/     |
      | /Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup/readonly/ |

  Scenario Outline: Error during backup due to missing partition column
    Given the customer_360_raw table does not contain the partition column "<missing_partition_column>"
    When the PySpark script attempts to partition by "<missing_partition_column>"
    Then the script fails with error message "Partition column <missing_partition_column> not found in source table"
    And no files are created in /Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup/

    Examples:
      | missing_partition_column |
      | state                   |
      | region                  |

  Scenario Outline: Error during backup due to unsupported compression type
    Given the compression type specified is "<compression_type>"
    When the PySpark script attempts to write parquet files with "<compression_type>" compression
    Then the script fails with error message "Compression type <compression_type> is not supported for parquet format"
    And no files are created in /Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup/

    Examples:
      | compression_type |
      | lz4             |
      | zstd            |
      | none            |

  Scenario: Successful vacuum operation retaining only records from the last 30 days
    Given the customer_360_raw table contains records with creation_date values ranging from past to present
    When the PySpark script performs a vacuum operation on purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw
    And deletes all records where creation_date < date_sub(<current_date>, 30)
    Then only records with creation_date >= date_sub(<current_date>, 30) remain in the table
    And all records older than 30 days are physically removed from the table
    And the operation does not affect the backup files in /Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup/

  Scenario Outline: Error during vacuum due to missing creation_date column
    Given the customer_360_raw table does not contain the column "<missing_date_column>"
    When the PySpark script attempts to filter records using "<missing_date_column>"
    Then the script fails with error message "Date column <missing_date_column> not found in source table"
    And no records are deleted from the table

    Examples:
      | missing_date_column      |
      | creation_date            |
      | last_interaction_date    |

  Scenario Outline: Error during vacuum due to invalid date format in creation_date
    Given the customer_360_raw table contains records with creation_date in format "<invalid_date_format>"
    When the PySpark script attempts to filter records for the last 30 days
    Then the script fails with error message "Invalid date format '<invalid_date_format>' in creation_date column"
    And no records are deleted from the table

    Examples:
      | invalid_date_format |
      | MM/dd/yyyy          |
      | dd-MM-yyyy          |
      | 2024/06/01          |

  Scenario: Data validation for backup - all columns and types are preserved
    Given the backup parquet files are created in /Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup/
    When the files are read back into a DataFrame
    Then the schema matches the source table:
      | column                | type    | nullable |
      | id                    | bigint  | true     |
      | name                  | string  | true     |
      | email                 | string  | true     |
      | phone                 | string  | true     |
      | company               | string  | true     |
      | job_title             | string  | true     |
      | address               | string  | true     |
      | city                  | string  | true     |
      | state                 | string  | true     |
      | country               | string  | true     |
      | industry              | string  | true     |
      | account_manager       | string  | true     |
      | creation_date         | date    | true     |
      | last_interaction_date | date    | true     |
      | purchase_history      | string  | true     |
      | notes                 | string  | true     |
      | zip                   | string  | true     |
    And the record count in backup matches the source table before vacuum

  Scenario Outline: Data-driven backup and vacuum validation for multiple states
    Given the customer_360_raw table contains records with state = "<state_value>" and creation_date = "<creation_date>"
    When the backup and vacuum operations are performed
    Then the backup parquet files are created in /Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup/state=<state_value>/
    And records with creation_date >= date_sub(<current_date>, 30) remain in the source table
    And records with creation_date < date_sub(<current_date>, 30) are deleted from the source table

    Examples:
      | state_value | creation_date  |
      | CA          | 2024-06-01     |
      | NY          | 2024-06-15     |
      | TX          | 2024-05-15     |
      | FL          | 2024-07-01     |

  Scenario: Error during backup due to insufficient storage space
    Given the backup location /Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup/ has less than required free space
    When the PySpark script attempts to write parquet files
    Then the script fails with error message "Insufficient storage space in /Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup/"
    And no partial files are left in the backup location

  Scenario: Error during vacuum due to lack of delete permissions
    Given the user executing the vacuum operation does not have delete permissions on purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw
    When the PySpark script attempts to delete records older than 30 days
    Then the script fails with error message "Permission denied: cannot delete records from purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw"
    And no records are deleted from the table

  Scenario: Backup and vacuum operations are atomic and do not leave partial results
    Given the backup and vacuum operations are initiated
    When an unexpected interruption occurs during backup or vacuum
    Then no partial parquet files are left in /Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup/
    And no partial deletes are performed in purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw
    And the operations can be retried safely

  Scenario: Backup does not alter data in the source table
    Given the backup operation is performed on purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw
    When the backup completes successfully
    Then the data in purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw remains unchanged before vacuum

  Scenario: Vacuum does not affect backup files
    Given the backup files are present in /Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup/
    When the vacuum operation is performed on purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw
    Then the backup files in /Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup/ are not modified or deleted

  Scenario: Error during backup due to null values in partition column
    Given the customer_360_raw table contains records with state = null
    When the PySpark script attempts to partition by state
    Then the script writes records with state = null to /Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup/state=null/
    And no error is thrown
    And all records are included in the backup

  Scenario: Error during backup due to duplicate id values
    Given the customer_360_raw table contains records with duplicate id values
    When the PySpark script performs the backup
    Then all records are backed up as-is
    And no error is thrown
    And the backup preserves duplicate id values

  Scenario: Error during vacuum due to invalid retention period
    Given the retention period specified is "<invalid_retention_period>"
    When the PySpark script attempts to filter records for vacuum
    Then the script fails with error message "Invalid retention period: <invalid_retention_period>"
    And no records are deleted from the table

    Examples:
      | invalid_retention_period |
      | -5                      |
      | 0                       |
      | thirty                  |
      | 999999                  |

